---
import BaseLayout from "../../../shared/components/BaseLayout.astro";
import { MAIN_SITE_URL } from "../../../shared/config/config";

const OPERATOR_EMAIL = "{OPERATOR-EMAIL}";

const jsonLd = {
  "@context": "https://schema.org",
  "@type": "WebPage",
  name: "Cookie Policy",
  url: MAIN_SITE_URL + "/cookie-policy/",
  description: "This site sets no cookies. Browser-local tools are explained.",
  isPartOf: { "@type": "WebSite", "name": "Porn Recovery Knowledge Platform", "url": "https://selfcontrolatlas.com" },
};
---
<BaseLayout
  siteTitle="Porn Recovery Knowledge Platform"
  siteUrl={MAIN_SITE_URL}
  title="Cookie Policy"
  description="This site sets no cookies. Browser-local tools are explained."
  canonical="/cookie-policy/"
  jsonLd={jsonLd}
  breadcrumbs={[{ name: "Home", url: "/" }, { name: "Cookie Policy", url: "/cookie-policy/" }]}
>
<h1>Cookie Policy</h1>
<p>This policy explains how this website uses cookies and similar browser storage. The
short version: <strong>this site does not use cookies.</strong> If you want to understand
what that means and why, read on.</p>
<p><strong>Last updated: 2026-10-23</strong></p>

<h2>1. What a cookie is</h2>
<p>A cookie is a small piece of data that a website can ask your browser to store and send
back on later visits. Cookies are commonly used for several purposes:</p>
<ul>
  <li><strong>Session management</strong> — keeping you logged in or tracking a session.</li>
  <li><strong>Preferences</strong> — remembering settings like language or layout.</li>
  <li><strong>Analytics</strong> — measuring how visitors use a site.</li>
  <li><strong>Advertising</strong> — targeting ads and measuring their performance.</li>
</ul>
<p>Cookies can be <strong>first-party</strong> (set by the site you are visiting) or
<strong>third-party</strong> (set by another domain, often for advertising or analytics
across sites).</p>

<h2>2. This site sets no cookies</h2>
<p>This website does <strong>not</strong> set cookies of any kind:</p>
<ul>
  <li><strong>No session cookies</strong> — there is no login or session to maintain.</li>
  <li><strong>No preference cookies</strong> — the site has no settings that persist across visits.</li>
  <li><strong>No tracking cookies</strong> — there is no analytics or tracking.</li>
  <li><strong>No advertising cookies</strong> — there is no ad network (see the <a href="/advertising-disclosure/">Advertising Disclosure</a>).</li>
  <li><strong>No third-party cookies</strong> — no embedded widgets, social buttons, or ad pixels from other domains.</li>
</ul>
<p>Because the site sets no cookies, there is no cookie consent banner, and none is needed.</p>

<h2>3. Why the site is cookie-free</h2>
<p>This is a deliberate design choice, and it follows directly from the site's privacy
posture:</p>
<ul>
  <li>The site has <strong>no accounts</strong>, so there is nothing to keep a session for.</li>
  <li>The site has <strong>no analytics</strong>, so there is nothing to track.</li>
  <li>The site has <strong>no advertising</strong>, so there is nothing to target.</li>
  <li>The site collects <strong>no personal data</strong> (see the <a href="/privacy-policy/">Privacy Policy</a>).</li>
</ul>
<p>When a site does not need to remember you, it does not need cookies.</p>

<h2>4. Local storage (browser-local tools)</h2>
<p>The optional <a href="/tools/trigger-journal/">Trigger Journal</a> tool uses
<strong>browser local storage</strong>, which is similar to a cookie but stays on your device:</p>
<ul>
  <li>Your entries are stored only in <strong>your own browser</strong>.</li>
  <li>They are <strong>never sent to any server</strong>.</li>
  <li>They are deleted when you clear your browser data.</li>
  <li>We have no access to them.</li>
</ul>
<p>Local storage used this way is a privacy-preserving alternative to storing your data on a
server. No local-storage data leaves your device.</p>

<h2>5. Cookies vs. local storage</h2>
<p>"Cookies" and "local storage" are both forms of browser storage, but they serve different
roles here:</p>
<table class="data-table">
  <thead><tr><th>Feature</th><th>Cookies on this site</th><th>Local storage (Trigger Journal)</th></tr></thead>
  <tbody>
    <tr><td>Who can read it</td><td>— (none exist)</td><td>Only your browser</td></tr>
    <tr><td>Sent to server?</td><td>—</td><td><strong>No</strong></td></tr>
    <tr><td>Persists after clearing?</td><td>—</td><td>No (cleared with browser data)</td></tr>
    <tr><td>Used for tracking?</td><td>—</td><td><strong>No</strong></td></tr>
    <tr><td>Purpose</td><td>—</td><td>Keeping your own journal entries on your device</td></tr>
  </tbody>
</table>
<p>The distinction matters: the site uses local storage only to keep your own data on your
own device, not to track you or remember anything about you.</p>

<h2>6. Third-party links</h2>
<p>Pages may link to external websites — research publishers, official classifications, or
crisis services. Those sites may set their own cookies according to their own policies,
which we do not control. When you leave this site, you are subject to the cookie policy of
the site you visit.</p>
<p>We link to external resources for reference; we do not cause them to set cookies on your
device, because we do not embed their content or run their scripts.</p>

<h2>7. How to control cookies and browser storage</h2>
<p>Because this site sets no cookies, there is nothing to manage here. But you can control
storage in your own browser generally:</p>
<ul>
  <li><strong>Clear site data</strong> — your browser's "clear browsing data" removes local storage, including Trigger Journal entries.</li>
  <li><strong>Private/incognito mode</strong> — prevents storage from persisting beyond the session.</li>
  <li><strong>Browser settings</strong> — you can block cookies and site data globally, though some sites (not this one) depend on cookies to function.</li>
</ul>
<p>If you clear your browser data, any Trigger Journal entries you created will be removed.
This is expected behavior, and it means your entries are gone from your device — which is
also how the tool stays private.</p>

<h2>8. What happens if you block storage</h2>
<p>Nothing on this site depends on cookies or local storage to function. If you block
cookies or disable local storage:</p>
<ul>
  <li>Every page still loads and reads normally.</li>
  <li>The only effect is that the Trigger Journal tool will not persist entries between sessions.</li>
</ul>

<h2>9. Do Not Track (DNT)</h2>
<p>This site respects the Do Not Track signal by virtue of not tracking at all. There is
nothing for a Do Not Track preference to disable, because no tracking exists.</p>

<h2>10. Frequently asked questions</h2>
<p><strong>Do I need to accept cookies to use this site?</strong> No. There are no cookies and no consent banner.</p>
<p><strong>Does the Trigger Journal use cookies?</strong> No, it uses local storage on your own device.</p>
<p><strong>Will external links set cookies?</strong> Possibly — linked third-party sites set their own cookies under their own policies.</p>
<p><strong>How do I delete my Trigger Journal data?</strong> Clear your browser's site data or browsing data.</p>

<h2>11. Changes to this policy</h2>
<p>If this policy changes, the updated version will be posted on this page, and the "Last
updated" date above will be revised. Because the site sets no cookies and has no accounts,
we cannot notify you individually.</p>

<h2>12. Contact</h2>
<p>Questions about this policy: <strong>{OPERATOR_EMAIL}</strong>, or the project repository
<code>https://github.com/martinxionbiotech-max/jiese/issues</code>.</p>
<p>Related documents: <a href="/privacy-policy/">Privacy Policy</a>,
<a href="/advertising-disclosure/">Advertising Disclosure</a>,
<a href="/disclaimer/">Disclaimer</a>.</p>
</BaseLayout>
